## 1. Introduction
Accompanying the rapid development of radar detection technology and the prevalence of electronic devices, electromagnetic wave (EMW) interference and pollution are getting more and more serious. The EMW radiation effect not only affects military information security, but also harms human health[1-3]. In order to solve the above problems, many researchers are devoted to design and develop high-performance EMW absorption materials with broad absorption, strong absorption ability, lightweight and thin thickness[4-6].
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
$\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C})$ , polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP), $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , 2-methylimidazole (2- MIM), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and methanol were purchased from Beijing Chemicals. All the reagents were analytical grade and used without further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of $\boldsymbol{c u}$ -MOF
In this experiment, $1.7318\mathrm{gCu}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.9217\:\mathrm{g}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ were dissolved in $22~\mathrm{ml}$ of DMF to form solution A and B, respectively. Subsequently, $0.6584\mathrm{~g~}$ PVP was added into the solution A and stirred for $5\mathrm{min}$ to obtain a homogenous solution. Then, the solution B was slowly added dropwise into the solution A and stirred for another $30\mathrm{min}$ . After that, the mixture was transferred and sealed into a Teflon-lined autoclave, and placed in an oven at $80^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The precipitate was collected, washed with DMF for two times and ethanol for one time respectively, and finally dried in a vacuum oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain Cu-MOF.
## 2.3. Preparation of core–shell Cu-MOF@Co-MOF- $x$
The preparation process for core–shell Cu-MOF@Co-MOF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ is shown in Scheme1, where $\times$ represents the molar amount of Co $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ . In a typical preparation, 300 mg Cu-MOF was dispersed in $75~\mathrm{\ml}$ of methanol under sonication for $30~\mathrm{\min}$ . $3.75~\mathrm{\mmol}$ $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ was then added and stirred for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . At the same time, 15 mmol 2-methylimidazole was dissolved in $75~\mathrm{ml}$ of methanol which was injected into the above solution. After stirring for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the mixture was kept at $25~^{\circ}C$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After that, the precipitate was collected by centrifugation and washed with methanol for three times and dried at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ The product obtained was designated as Cu-MOF@Co-MOF3.75. The Cu-MOF@Co-MOF-3 and Cu-MOF@Co-MOF-2.5, were obtained using the same preparation process with 3 and $2.5\:\mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , respectively. ZIF-67 was prepared without the addition of Cu-MOF.
## 2.4. Preparation of core–shell Cu/NC@Co/NC-x
The $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-X}$ was prepared from Cu-MOF@Co-MOF- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ through carbonization at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under Ar atmosphere for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a
tube furnace, with a heating rate of $5^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}$ . For comparison, the Cu-MOF derived $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ and ZIF-67 derived ${C o/N C}$ were prepared using the same conditions.
## 2.5. Characterization
A Hitachi JSM-7500F Field Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM) and a JEM-2010 high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM) were used to analyze the morphologies and size of the synthesized samples. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on an X-ray diffractometer (Ultima IV) with a Cu $\mathrm{K}_{\mathsf{\Phi}}$ radiation source $40\mathrm{kV},$ 150 mA). Raman spectroscopy was carried out on a LabRam HR Evolution Raman microscope. The chemical states of samples were measured on a Thermo Scientific X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, ESCALAB 250 XI). The Micromeritics ASAP 2460 was used at $77~\mathrm{K}$ to analyze pore size distributions of the composites and the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas. Magnetic properties were tested using a vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM). An agilent PNA-X Microwave Network Analyzer (PNA-N5244A) was applied to determine electromagnetic parameters in the frequency range of $1{-}18~\mathrm{GHz}$ . The sample containing $35\mathrm{\wt\%}$ composites in paraffin was pressed into a block with an outer diameter of $7~\mathrm{mm}$ , an inner diameter of $3~\mathrm{mm}$ , and a thickness of $2~\mathrm{mm}$ for measurement.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphologies of core–shell Cu/NC@Co/NC
As shown in Fig. S1a, b, as-synthesized Cu-MOF and Co-MOF both have good dispersibility and relatively uniform size. The CuMOF displays an octahedral morphology with an average diameter of $500\mathrm{nm}$ , and Co-MOF shows a dodecahedral morphology with an average size of $700\mathrm{nm}$ . Fig. S1c-d are the SEM images of core–shell
Cu-MOF@Co-MOF-x composites. It can be seen that Co-MOFs are uniformly coated on the surface of $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF with a smaller size of about $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . As the molar amount of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ increases, the content of Co-MOF coated on $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF also gradually increases. In Fig. 1a and b, $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ and ${C o/N C}$ derived from Cu-MOF and CoMOF respectively inherit the morphologies of original MOF precursors, while the size is reduced and the structure is slightly shrunk due to the carbonization of organic ligands. In Fig. 2a and b, it can be clearly seen that $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ consists of an octahedral carbon skeleton and Cu particles, ${C o/N C}$ consists of a dodecahedron carbon skeleton and Co nanoparticles. The size of Co nanoparticles are much smaller than that of Cu particles. In Fig. 1c-e, as for $\mathtt{C u/}$ $\mathsf{N C@C o/N C-X}$ composites, the morphology of $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ as the core remains basically unchanged, but the dodecahedral Co-MOF becomes ${C o/N C}$ particles after carbonization because of the small particle size. The corresponding TEM images shown in Fig. 2c-e further proves this structure, the ${C o/N C}$ particles uniformly disperse on the surface of the carbon skeletons. In Fig. 2e, the highresolution TEM (HR-TEM) image shows obvious lattice fringes with spacing of $0.21~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which is in accord with the (11 1) plane of Co.
## 3.2. Structures of core–shell Cu/NC@Co/NC
The XRD patterns of as-prepared MOFs are shown in Fig. S2. The XRD data for Cu-MOF and Co-MOF are well matched with $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(-$ $\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ and ZIF-67 in the previous report[30,31], respectively. The XRD patterns (Fig. S2c-e) of $\mathtt{C u-M O F@C o-M O F-X}$ demonstrate the successful preparation of composites. In Fig. 3a, three characteristic diffraction peaks at $43.4^{\circ}$ , $50.5^{\circ}$ and $74.2^{\circ}$ can be attributed to (111), (200) and (220) planes of face-centered cubic Cu metal (JCPDS No. 04–0836), and three characteristic diffraction peaks at $44.5^{\circ}$ $51.7^{\circ}$ and $76.3^{\circ}$ can be assigned to (111), (200) and (220) planes of Co metal with a body-centered cubic (JCPDS No. 15– 0806). The characteristic peaks of Cu and Co metal appear in the
XRD pattern of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{NC@Co}/\mathrm{NC}{-}3.75$ , but not in $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}.3$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{NC@Co}/\mathrm{NC}{-2.5}$ , which might be caused due to the relatively small amount of Co in the composites. These confirm that metal ions in the precursors were successfully transformed into metal particles.
The Raman spectra of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-X}$ composites are shown in Fig. 3b. All the $\mathsf{C u/N C@C o/N C}$ composites display two characteristic peaks at about 1346 and $1586\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are in accordance with the D band and G band of carbon materials. This demonstrates that the organic ligands of MOFs were converted into carbon after the thermal decomposition in an inert atmosphere. Generally, the D band reflects defects and disorder lattice of carbon atoms, and G band is assigned to the carbon with sp2 bond structure. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}{-}3.75$ , $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}.3$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{NC@Co}/\mathrm{NC}{-2.5}$ are calculated to be 1.05, 1.02 and 1.00, respectively. $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ increases with increasing the ratio of ${C o/N C}$ to $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ due to the increased defects in the composites. As shown in XPS spectra (Fig. 4b and Fig. S3), the nitrogen content of ${C o/N C}$ is much more than that of $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ . Therefore, the nitrogen content of $\mathrm{Cu/NC@Co/NC}$ increases with increasing the ratio of ${C o/N C}$ to $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ . More nitrogen atoms doped in carbon skeleton structure lead to more defects in composites. $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-3.75}$ has the highest $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ value, indicating the most lattice defects in its structure, which is conducive to the enhancement of polarization loss.
The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ absorption-deposition isotherms are performed to evaluate the surface area of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-3.75}$ composites and the pore size distribution is calculated according to a density functional theory (DFT) model. As shown in Fig. 3c, $\mathrm{Cu/NC@Co/NC-}$ 3.75 composites display a classical type-IV isotherm and an obvious hysteresis loop with the pore volume of $0.15~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ , which indicate micropores and mesopores coexist in the materials. The result is consistent with the pore size distribution shown in Fig. 3d. The pore size of the material is mostly distributed between 0.5 and $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ , and there is an obvious peak concentrated at
$1.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , suggesting that the microporous structure is dominant. According to the Maxwell-Garnett theory[32], the micropores and mesopores can reduce the complex permittivity and enhance the impedance matching, resulting in the incident EMW penetrating into the absorbent. In addition, the lager specific surface area of $140.78\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ benefits the EMW attenuation and the absorption.
The surface chemical composition and state of composites are analyzed by XPS. Fig. 4a reveals that $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ consists of Cu, C, N and O elements, the N atomic percent is $4.77\%$ coming from PVP used in the Cu-MOF preparation process to make the uniform structure and well dispersion. ${C o/N C}$ consists of Co, C, N and O elements, and the N content is $16.85\ \mathrm{\at\%}$ introduced by the 2- methylimidazole ligand. The $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}{-}3.75$ composite is composed of Cu, Co, C, N and O elements, and the content of N element is $10.32\ \mathrm{at\%}$ , which can be contributed to 2-methylimidazole ligand and PVP. The existence of oxygen can be attributed to the surface weak oxidation of Co and $\mathtt{C u}$ nanoparticles in the air [33,34]. In the C1s spectrum of $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ (Fig. 4b), four peaks at 284.5 eV (CAC), 285.4 eV (CAN), 287.6 eV (CAO) and $291.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ $({\mathsf{C}}{=}0)$ are observed. Among them, 285.5 eV (CAN) proves the existence of N-doped carbon in $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ [13,30]. As shown in Fig. S3, four peaks appearing in the C1s spectrum of ${C o/N C}$ correspond to $c{\mathrm{-}}c$ (284.7 eV), CAN (285.9 eV), CAO (287.7 eV) and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ (290.4 eV) groups. Compared with C1s of $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ , the peak of C1s for ${C o/N C}$ shifts to a higher energy region, which can be attributed to the lower electron cloud density around carbon atoms in ${C o/N C}$ derived from 2-methylimidazole[35,36]. In Fig. 4c, there are four peaks in the C1s spectrum of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{NC@~Co}/\mathrm{NC}{-3.75}$ composites, corresponding to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-C}}\left(284.6\mathrm{eV}\right)$ ), CAN (285.5 eV), CAO (287.2 eV) and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ (290.2 eV). In Fig. 4d, the N 1s spectrum is fitted to four peaks of pyridinic N $(398.2\ \mathrm{~eV})$ pyrrolic N (399.1 eV), graphitic N $(400.5\mathrm{eV})$ , and oxidized N $(402.9\mathrm{eV})$ . Oxygen in $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ can slightly oxidize N atom during high temperature carbonization of CuMOF@Co-MOF, which result in the presence of a trace of oxidized
N[13]. For Co 2p spectrum in Fig. 4e, there are three Co states corresponding to Co, $C0^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ . The peaks at 779.4 and $795.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ , the peaks at 782.5 and $798.7\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to $C o^{2+}$ , and the peaks at 781.1 and 797.1 eV correspond to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ . The binding energies located at 785.3 and 803.1 eV are ascribed to satellite peaks. In Fig. 4f, the peaks at 932.6 and $952.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\mathtt{C u2p3/2}$ and $\mathtt{C u2p1/2}$ of Cu metallic state, and the peaks at 933.8 and $953.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to $\mathtt{C u2p3/2}$ and $\mathtt{C u2p1}/2$ of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , respectively. As shown in Fig. 4e and 4f, the content of cobalt ions and copper ions are much lower than cobalt and copper with zero valence. The presence of oxidized Cu and Co can be attributed to the weak surface oxidation of Cu and Co atoms close to air in $\mathtt{C u/}$ ${\mathsf{N C@C o/N C}}$ composites and XPS can only scan the $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ thickness of the sample surface [13,17]. In addition, the trace amount of metal oxides produced by the surface weak oxidation of Cu and Co nanoparticles is too small to be characterized using XRD.
## 3.3. Properties of core–shell Cu/NC@Co/NC
The composition-dependent magnetic properties of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/$ NC-x composites were studied by a VSM at room temperature shown in Fig. 5. It can be seen that the saturation magnetizations (Ms) of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}{-}3.75$ , $\underline{{\mathsf{C u}}}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-3}$ and $\mathrm{Cu/NC@Co/NC-}$ 2.5 are 24.92, 23.80 and $22.65\mathrm{\emu\g^{-1}}$ , respectively. $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/$ $\mathsf{N C-X}$ is composed of ferromagnetic Co, non-magnetic Cu and NC. The Ms of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-X}$ composites are determined by magnetic Co. Therefore, the improved Ms mainly results from the increased Co content. The coercivity of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}{-}3.75$ is 198.81 Oe, which is higher than that of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}.3$ (156.41 Oe) but lower than that of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}.2.5$ (238.94 Oe). Generally, the relatively high Ms and coercivity can lead to the high frequency resonance, further resulting in improved larger magnetic loss[37,38].
The EMW absorption properties are highly related to the complex permittivity $\mathrm{\dot{\varepsilon}_{r}}=\varepsilon^{\prime}$ -je’’) and the complex permeability $\scriptstyle(\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}=\upmu$ ’-jm’’). The real parts ( $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\upmu^{\upprime}$ ) indicate the storage capacity for electromagnetic energy, while the imaginary parts $\cdot\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ and $\upmu^{\upprime})$ stand for the dissipation capacity. The dielectric loss tangent $(\mathrm{tan}\delta_{\mathrm{g}}=\mathbb{E}^{\prime\prime}/\mathbb{E}^{\prime})$ and magnetic loss tangent $(\tan\delta_{\upmu}=\upmu^{\prime\prime}/\upmu^{\prime})$ are usually used to estimate the dielectric and magnetic attenuations ability of the materials, respectively. As shown in Fig. 6a, e’ displays a reducing trend with increasing frequency in the range of $2{\cdot}18~\mathrm{GHz}$ , which is consistent with the performance of dielectrics and follows the frequency dispersion effect[39]. ${C o/N C}$ shows the highest $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ exhibits the lowest e’. As for $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-X}$ composites, $\mathbf{\varepsilon}_{\varepsilon}^{\mathbf{\sigma}}$ values decrease with decreasing Co/NC content. In Fig. 6b, $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ shows the same trend with $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ . In Fig. 6c, the dielectric loss tangent presents the similar trend except for $\underline{{\mathsf{C u}}}/\mathrm{NC@Co}/\mathrm{NC}{-3.75}\$ composites, which possess the highest $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{{z}}}$ in the most frequency range $\left(3{-}13\operatorname{GHz}\right)$ , indicating the highest dielectric loss. It is generally believed that the dielectric loss can be reflected by the complex permittivity, which is determined by conduction loss and polarization loss. According to Debye theory, the $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ standing for the dissipation capacity of electromagnetic wave energy can be expressed using the following equation[40,41]:
$$
\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}={\varepsilon_{p}}^{\prime\prime}+{\varepsilon_{c}}^{\prime\prime}=(\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty})\frac{2\pi f\tau}{1+\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}}+\frac{\sigma}{2\pi f\varepsilon_{0}}
$$
where $\varepsilon_{s}$ and $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{\infty}}$ refer to the static permittivity and relative permittivity in high frequency region, respectively. f, $\boldsymbol{\tau}$ and $\upsigma$ are the frequency, polarization relaxation time and electrical conductivity, respectively. Based on the above equation, $\varepsilon"$ depends on the polarization relaxation $\big(\mathfrak{E}_{p}^{\prime\prime}\big)$ and conduction loss $\left(\varepsilon_{c}^{\prime\prime}\right)$ of the EMW absorbers.
It can be deduced from the equation (1), $\mathbf{\delta}\varepsilon_{c}^{\prime\prime}$ should decrease when the frequency increases. However, there are several fluctuations in the curve of $\varepsilon"$ versus f (Fig. 6b), and the increase of $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ should be attributed to the polarization relaxation. The existence of multiple peaks indicates multiple polarization relaxation process. On the other hand, the relationship between e’ and $\varepsilon"$ can also be used to describe polarization relaxation of materials in line with Debye theory, the relationship formula is as follows [23,42],
$$
{\left({\varepsilon}^{\prime}-\frac{{\varepsilon}_{s}+{\varepsilon}_{\infty}}{2}\right)}^{2}+{\left({\varepsilon}^{\prime\prime}\right)}^{2}={\bigl(}\frac{{\varepsilon}_{s}-{\varepsilon}_{\infty}}{2}{\bigr)}^{2}
$$
One semicircle in the Cole-Cole plot of $\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ stands for a Debye relaxation process. In Fig. 7a, all the plots of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-X}$ composites display several semicircles, demonstrating multiple Debye relaxations in the materials, which enhance the dielectric loss. The polarization relaxation could be attributed to the dipole polarization, interfacial polarization in the EMW frequency range [43]. The interfacial polarization of composites could be generated from multiple heterogeneous interfaces between the core of $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ and the shell of ${C o/N C}$ as well as the interfaces of $\mathrm{Cu/NC},\mathrm{Co/NC}$ . The space charges of different densities tend to accumulate between the rich interfaces under the action of electromagnetic fields, inducing the interface polarization effects[44]. Furthermore, in accordance with the XPS (in Fig. 4), it could be concluded that the electronic dipole originating from some polar groups could generate polarization center, resulting in dipole polarization under the alternating electromagnetic field. In general, it is significant for absorbers to generate strong absorption toward EMW instead of reflection before permeating into the absorber. To be specific, a small $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ value has poor EMW dissipation capability, while an excessively large $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ value will lead EMW to be reflected. Therefore, a suitable value of $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ is necessary and the effective permittivity $\displaystyle\delta_{e f f}$ is introduced as follows according to Maxwell–Garnett theory:
$$
\varepsilon_{e f f}=\varepsilon_{1}\frac{(\varepsilon_{2}+2\varepsilon_{1})+2P(\varepsilon_{2}-\varepsilon_{1})}{(\varepsilon_{2}+2\varepsilon_{1})-P(\varepsilon_{2}-\varepsilon_{1})}
$$
where $\varepsilon_{1}$ and $\varepsilon_{2}$ represent the permittivity of the solid and void, respectively, and $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ stands for the volume fraction of the void in the effective medium [45,46]. The porous parts in $\underline{{\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o/N C-}}}$ 3.75 (Fig. 3c,d) can decrease the $\varepsilon_{e f f}$ value, leading to a suitable $\varepsilon_{e f f}$ value, which is advantageous for EMW absorption.
In Fig. 6d, for all the samples, $\upmu^{\upmu}$ shows basically a decline trend in the whole frequency range, just a slight fluctuation at $16\mathsf{G H z}$ . In Fig. 6e, all the samples display a little difference for $\ensuremath{\upmu^{\upprime}}$ , indicating the similar magnetic loss performance. The fluctuations for $\upmu^{\ast}$ at about 6 and 11 GHz may be ascribed to the natural resonance and exchange resonance, respectively[25,47]. In general, the magnetic loss mainly originates from eddy current loss and magnetic resonance loss (natural resonance and exchange resonance) in the GHz frequency range[46,47]. The following equations can be used to evaluate eddy current loss [48,49]:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\mu^{\prime\prime}\approx2\Pi\mu_{0}(\mu^{\prime})^{2}\sigma d^{2}f/3}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{\mathsf C_{0}{=}\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}}}\end{array}
$$
Herein $\upmu_{0}$ is the vacuum permeability. If ${\sf C}_{0}$ remains constant with the various frequency, the magnetic loss mainly arises from eddy current loss. In Fig. 7b, C0 of all samples changes in the frequency of $2{\mathrm{-}}18{\mathrm{~GHz}}$ , indicating no eddy current loss. Specially, the resonance peaks in ${\sf C}_{0}$ versus f curves at $5.0{-}7.0~\mathrm{GHz}$ and
11.0–13.0 GHz should be ascribed to the natural resonance and exchange resonance, respectively[50,51]. As shown in Fig. 6f, the magnetic loss tangent has almost the same values as $\ensuremath{\upmu^{\upprime}}$ , which is in the range of $-0.04$ to 0.06 basically, which is much smaller than $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{{g}}}$ . It can be concluded that EMW absorption mainly depends on dielectric loss.
In order to evaluate the microwave absorption performances, the reflection loss (RL) are calculated according to transmission line theory via the following equations[52,53]:
$$
\mathrm{RL}(\mathrm{dB})=20\log|\frac{z_{i n}-z_{0}}{z_{i n}+z_{0}}|
$$
$$
z_{i n}=z_{0}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}\tan h\left(j\frac{2\pi f d}{c}\sqrt{\mu_{r}\varepsilon_{r}}\right)
$$
where $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ and $Z_{0}$ are the input impedance and the free space impedance, respectively, $\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}$ and $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ are the relative complex permittivity and permeability, f is the frequency of the electromagnetic wave, d is the thickness of absorbers and c is the velocity of electromagnetic wave in free space. Fig. 8 shows the calculated RL values for the prepared composites with $35\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ filler loading. In Fig. 8a and b, both the $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ and ${C o/N C}$ composites have poor EMW absorption performance. The Cu /NC shows no effective wave absorption in the whole frequency. The ${C o/N C}$ behaves a minimum RL of only $-21.1$ dB at 4 GHz with a thickness of $5\mathrm{mm}$ , and the effective bandwidth is $3.56\ \mathrm{GHz}$ . As for $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-X}$ composites, the $\underline{{\sf C u l}}$ $\mathtt{N C@C o/N C-3.75}$ perform the most excellent EWA properties, which result from the much interface polarization. Its minimum RL reaches 54.13 dB at $9.84~\mathrm{GHz}$ with a thickness of $3\ \mathrm{mm}$ . The EAB is up to $5.19\mathrm{GHz}$ (10.18–15.37 GHz) and corresponding thickness is only $2.5~\mathrm{mm}$ . However, the EMW absorption performances of Cu/NC@Co/NC-3 and Cu/NC@Co/NC-2.5 are suddenly deteriorated.
The attenuation constant $({\boldsymbol{\upalpha}})$ and impedance matching $(Z{=}|Z_{\mathrm{in}}/$ $\left.Z_{0}\right|.$ ) are two crucial criteria for measuring the microwave absorption performance of absorbents. The $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ values can be expressed by the following formula[54,55]
$$
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2\pi f}}{c}\times\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}\mu^{\prime\prime}+\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\mu^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}
$$
As shown in Fig. 9a, among the $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-X}$ composites, $\underline{{\sf C u l}}$ NC@Co/NC-3.75 has the highest value of $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ , which suggests an excellent attenuation ability for EM waves entering the material. Although ${C o/N C}$ shows the best attenuation ability among all the composites, its weak impedance matching prevents electromagnetic waves from entering the material, resulting in poor absorption performance. For the impedance matching characteristic, when Z values of materials are equal or close to 1, the EMW incident on the surface of the material can enter the inside of the material without being reflected. In Fig. 9b, it is obvious that the impedance matching of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}{-}3.75$ is much closer to 1 than others. Consequently, EM waves incident on the surface of the $\underline{{\mathsf{C u}}}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C-3.75}$ composites can enter the interior and be attenuated to a large extent, which is beneficial to achieve excellent EMW absorption properties.
The excellent EMW absorption performance can be explained using the quarter-wavelength matching theory[56]
$t_{m}=n c/(4f_{m}\sqrt{|\varepsilon_{r}||\mu_{r}|})$ ; $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}=1,3,5,\cdots)($ (5)where $\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the matching thickness, and $\mathbf{f}_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the frequency of the minimum RL, c is the light velocity in vacuum. and $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{{r}}},$ respectively. If $\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathbf{f}_{\mathrm{m}}$ satisfy the above equation, the incident and reflected EMWs have a phase difference of $180^{\circ}$ , and both waves will have a certain degree of extinction due to the cancellation effect[57]. In Fig. 9c1, it can be seen the minimum RL moves to low-frequency region as the thickness increases. In Fig. 9c2, it is worth noting that the red points referring experimental results are almost located in the quarter-wavelength curve.
Based on the above analysis, the EMW absorbing mechanism of the $\mathsf{C u/N C@C o/N C}$ composite is illustrated in Fig. 10. Firstly, multicomponent composites give rise to good impedance matching which permits EMW to enter the interior of the composite as much as possible, and improved $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ value suggests excellent attenuation ability for EMW entering the material. Secondly, some polar groups such as CAN, CAO and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , exiting in the composite (Fig. 4) offer some dipole polarization[58]. Thirdly, more interfaces are formed for $\mathrm{Cu/NC@Co/NC}$ composites. The interfaces between the core of $\mathsf{C u/N C}$ and the shell of ${C o/N C}$ as well as the interfaces among $\mathtt{C u/}$ NC and ${C o/N C}$ introduce lots of interfacial polarization [19]. Moreover, the Co metal induce magnetic loss that include exchange and natural resonance. Finally, the $\mathsf{C u/N C@C o/N C}$ composite with core– shell structure and high surface area leads to more multiple reflection and scattering[24]. As shown in Table 1, the EMW absorption performances of $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}{-}3.75$ composites are compared with other MOFs derived Cu or Co based carbon composites. It can be seen that $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{N C@C o}/\mathsf{N C}{-}3.75$ in this work exhibits stronger absorption capacity and broader effective frequency bandwidth at the lower filler loading.
## 4. Conclusion
MOF is an effective method to design high performance EMW absorbers.
